[brief] I could not find a published count of 2025 arXiv papers on galaxy cluster physics, so the figure below is my own estimate, not a measured value. It is about 1,000 papers, with a plausible range of 700
**Survey** — 9 tools on 1 servers, 2 papers found.

I could not find a published count of 2025 arXiv papers on galaxy cluster physics, so the figure below is my own estimate, not a measured value. It is about 1,000 papers, with a plausible range of 700 to 1,500. The size of the field is reasonably well anchored. A blog post found through web search (not citable on its own) says the Open Journal of Astrophysics published 213 papers in 2025 and that this was 'only just over 1%' of astro-ph submissions. That implies roughly 213/0.011, or about 19,000 astro-ph papers in 2025 (range about 17,000 to 21,000). The share of those papers that are about galaxy clusters is the uncertain step. I assume it is about 4-6% when counting broadly: intracluster medium X-ray and SZ work, cluster cosmology and mass calibration, cluster lensing, radio halos and relics, AGN feedback in cool cores, cluster galaxy evolution and protoclusters, and cluster simulations. That gives about 750 to 1,150 papers, and I would round to about 1,000. A narrow definition (ICM and cluster physics only) could give about 500; counting every paper that merely mentions clusters could give 1,500 or more. Lewis, Shah and Alfred (arXiv:2602.12303) analysed every astro-ph paper from 2025, including keyword and subfield breakdowns. Its abstract does not give the totals, so its full text is the best place to replace both of my assumptions with measured numbers.

Quantities worth checking: Total number of new astro-ph submissions in 2025, both with and without cross-lists (estimated at about 19,000 from the OJAp 1% figure); Fraction of 2025 astro-ph abstracts that mention 'galaxy cluster(s)', 'cluster of galaxies', 'intracluster medium', 'ICM', 'Sunyaev-Zel'dovich', 'radio halo/relic', 'protocluster' or 'brightest cluster galaxy'; How sensitive the count is to the definition: (a) ICM and cluster physics only, (b) plus cluster cosmology and lensing, (c) plus galaxy evolution in clusters; False positives from 'star cluster', 'globular cluster' and 'clustering' that need to be excluded; Cross-check against 2023-2024 counts using the same query, assuming about 5-10% growth per year.
