**Plan** — 1 lines of investigation:
1. Turn the contested 2025 base counts into validated citations, then try to replace the assumed galaxy-cluster share with a measured or figure-read number. Use arXiv:2602.12303 (Figs. 12-13) as the main source and arXiv:2511.12353 as a secondary anchor for per-year totals and any cluster concept. End with a strict and a broad estimate of 2025 galaxy-cluster papers, each with explicit arithmetic. — Critiques c-02 and c-03 contest f-001, f-002 and f-003. The 18,660 total, the exclusion of cross-submissions and the Table 1 counts were never validated with cite(), and the cluster share is still the survey's assumption. No ADS, arXiv-API count or coding tool is in the catalogue. That leaves three routes to a measured share: reading the extracted keyword and subfield figures of 2602.12303, finding a cluster or ICM concept count in AstroMLab 5, and finding citable bibliometric statements. This single direction covers the verification work (d-05) and the figure reading (d-04). It also does the trend check that c-01 and c-02 asked for, so the final answer rests on sourced numbers wherever the literature allows.
Critic's view: accept — Step 4's upper bound is weak. A cluster term missing from Fig. 12's top-N list only bounds that exact keyword string, not the share of cluster-topic papers under the strict or broad definition. A paper can be about clusters without carrying the literal keyword, and keyword vocabularies split one topic across many terms (ICM, SZ, cluster lensing). Present the smallest-bar value as a bound on single-keyword frequency only. Do not use it to cap the strict or broad estimate.; Values read off a figure image cannot be passed to cite() as verbatim quotes. Cite the caption text only. Label any bar value as 'figure-read, approximate, plus or minus one tick' in the synthesis, not as a sourced number. If the image is not legible, record 'not readable' rather than estimating a value.; Step 6 has two problems with the AstroMLab 5 concept share. First, concepts are fine-grained and a paper can carry several, so summing several cluster-related concepts double-counts papers. Second, the dataset spans decades, so an all-years share is dominated by older work and may not match the 2025 topic mix. Use only one concept count per estimate, or state that the summed share is an upper bound. Present the result as an independent cross-check on the assumed 4-6%, not as a 2025 measurement..
